When did this become "the usual strategical tip"? I remember, maybe 18
months ago, when Maniac's Ascension Guide was just an idea to provide
a "walkthrough" for Nethack, the "protection racket" was hugely
controversial, and it seemed to me, opposed by most of the more
experienced players around here.

Since then, it seems that this strategy is being advocated by the
majority of participants here. Most everyone concedes that the
strategy seems to work reasonably well for very weak character classes
who start out with a lot of money--gnomish healers, in particular,
since they don't have a lot of trouble with the usual denizens of the
Mines and start out with healing potions to beef up their HP. But I'm
getting the feeling that most relative newbies here think that This Is
the Way to Beat the Game, and use it for every character class.

The idea is seductive, and I've tried it many times, but with little
success. I find that even if you can successfully get to the Minetown
priest and donate for several levels of protection, you're still a
puny character deep in the dungeons that can be killed with one
unlucky thwack. It's too easy to lose the pet you're depending on, or
fall through a trap door, or by some other means get yourself into a
world of trouble you're not prepared to deal with.

Most, if not all, of my successful characters did Sokoban before the
Mines. Donating for AC came later, in midgame, as I found treasure
zoos, leprechaun halls, and other stores of major loot. To some
degree, it's a personal style issue, and I have no quibbles with
anyone who happens to succeed at it. But people should know that it's
a *very* debatable strategy.
